INT. CHURCH BASEMENT - BINGO NIGHT - EVENING

A low room of folding tables and fluorescent tubes. Cards, daubers, a coffee urn. A raffle drum sits on a stage made of risers. Above it hangs a banner: ST. ANSELM'S ROOF FUND.

WILHELMINA OKAFOR-DUNN (70s, church-lady hat, a ring of keys) holds the microphone. DOV KESSLER (60s, cardigan, the treasurer, anxious hands) counts bills at the cash table. ANSELM PRICE (40s, big man, usher's lanyard) stacks chairs by the wall. CANDACE LOWE (30s, nurse's scrubs under a coat, new in the parish) sits alone with a single card. RUTENDO MOYO (20s, sharp, laptop bag, a visitor from the diocese) stands at the stairs, watching them all.

WILHELMINA
Good evening, everyone. Welcome. We'll begin in two minutes.

Dov glances up at Rutendo, then down at his cash again.

DOV
(to himself)
Four hundred. Four hundred and ten.

RUTENDO
Mr. Kessler, I'd like to see the ledger before the draw.

DOV
It's bingo night. The books are in the office.

RUTENDO
Then let's go to the office.

DOV
After the game.

Wilhelmina lowers the mic.

WILHELMINA
Dov, is there a problem?

DOV
No problem, Willie.

ANSELM
(to the room)
Folks, take your seats, please. We're starting soon.

Candace moves to a different table, nearer the stairs. Rutendo notices.

RUTENDO
You're new, aren't you?

CANDACE
Three months.

RUTENDO
Why bingo?

CANDACE
It's quiet. I work nights. Sometimes I need quiet.

Dov stuffs the cash into a metal box and locks it. His hands are shaking.

RUTENDO
Mr. Kessler. The ledger.

DOV
(louder)
I said after the game!

Heads turn. The urn hisses. Wilhelmina steps off the riser, keys jingling.

WILHELMINA
Dov Kessler. Look at me.

DOV
Willie, don't.

WILHELMINA
I have known you thirty-one years. Look at me.

Dov looks. His face crumples.

DOV
(barely audible)
The roof isn't getting fixed.

ANSELM
What did he say?

WILHELMINA
(to Anselm, flat)
Sit down.

ANSELM
Wilhelmina, what did he say?

WILHELMINA
He said the roof isn't getting fixed.

A long silence. Candace puts down her dauber.

CANDACE
(to the room)
How much?

DOV
Eleven thousand. Over two years.

ANSELM
(to himself, muttering)
Eleven thousand. Eleven thousand dollars.

RUTENDO
Where did it go, Mr. Kessler?

DOV
My wife's treatments. The insurance wouldn't...

He can't finish. Wilhelmina takes the metal box from the table and holds it against her chest.

WILHELMINA
(to everyone)
Nobody leaves. Nobody calls anyone. We are going to have this out like a family.

ANSELM
(rising, loud)
Family? He stole from us!

CANDACE
Anselm, sit.

ANSELM
Don't tell me what to do. You've been here ninety days.

CANDACE
I've been here long enough to know a man in trouble when I see one.

ANSELM
He lied to every person in this room!

DOV
(weeping)
I know. I know I did.

RUTENDO
(to Wilhelmina)
Mrs. Okafor-Dunn, I have a report due to the bishop.

WILHELMINA
Then write that the church of St. Anselm found its own answer.

RUTENDO
What answer?

Wilhelmina walks to the riser, sets the cash box on the raffle drum and opens it. She takes out the whole stack of bills and holds it up.

WILHELMINA
(to the crowd)
Tonight's pot goes to Dov's wife. All of it.

ANSELM
That's the roof money!

WILHELMINA
The roof can leak another winter. Dov can't.

Anselm opens his mouth. Closes it. He drags his hand down his face.

ANSELM
(to himself)
Lord, give me patience.

He walks to the cash table, pulls out his wallet, and drops two twenties on the pile.

ANSELM (CONT'D)
That's mine.

CANDACE
(softly)
Mine too.

She adds a ten. Rutendo stares at them. Then, with a long breath, she closes her laptop bag.

RUTENDO
My report will say the ledger was in order.

Dov covers his face. Wilhelmina lifts the microphone.

WILHELMINA
B-seven. B, seven.

The room breathes. Daubers lift. Dov sits, shoulders shaking, as the first ball rattles in the drum.
